{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (new): `_fnv1a` is documented as "32-bit FNV-1a" but masks the accumulator to 31 bits**

The doc (line 119-123) and the name claim FNV-1a 32-bit, but the update step ends with `& 0x7fffffff` — bit 31 is dropped every round, so this is FNV-1a mod 2^31, not the standard 32-bit variant. Nothing is wrong functionally: the split-multiply is verified exact (all intermediates stay below 2^53 — `(hash & 0xffff) * prime` < 2^40, `(hash >>> 16) * prime` < 2^39, the `& 0xffff` recombination is mod-2^32-correct, so VM and dart2js agree bit-for-bit), and determinism — not FNV conformance — is the actual requirement. One-line doc fix so a future reader doesn't "correct" the mask into a real 32-bit variant (or port the algorithm elsewhere expecting standard FNV-1a digests):

```dart
/// FNV-1a, folded to 31 bits (…): the 0x7fffffff mask keeps every
/// intermediate below 2^53 for bit-exact dart2js arithmetic …
